<section class="page-head">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Hackerly</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px">
      A hackathon platform: discovery and participation for hackers, operations for organizers,
      a focused review workspace for judges. One identity system, one database, one set of rules —
      three different ways of using it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="doc" id="what">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <p>
        A hacker can find a hackathon, read it, register, join or create a team, build a project and
        submit it before a deadline the server enforces. An organizer can create a hackathon, brand
        it, set dates and prizes, define tracks and submission fields, publish a weighted rubric,
        invite judges, assign projects, watch judging progress, compute results and publish them. A
        judge can sign in, see their assignments, open a project, score it against the rubric,
        explain the score, submit, and move to the next one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="judging">How judging works</h2>
      <p>
        Each review is converted to a 0–100 score using the organizer&rsquo;s rubric weights. A
        project&rsquo;s score is the mean of its submitted reviews. That is the whole default method,
        and it is what <code>judging_mode = raw</code> means.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <code>judging_mode = normalized</code>, two extra steps run. First, each judge&rsquo;s
        mean deviation from the panel mean is measured, and a project&rsquo;s score is shifted by
        <code>λ</code> times the average offset of the judges who reviewed it. λ = 0 removes nothing;
        λ = 1 removes the full panel-difficulty effect. Second, the adjusted scores are min–max
        scaled to 0–100 <em>inside each track</em>, because two tracks can have very different
        score distributions and one should not dominate the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        The method is deliberately conservative and fully deterministic. It is <strong>not</strong>
        a claim of statistical superiority, and it is not validated against any external ground
        truth. What it does guarantee: identical scores, a single reviewer, missing reviews, uneven
        judge workloads, zero variance and exact ties all produce a defined, explainable output.
        Ties share a rank and the next rank is skipped, and ties break deterministically on review
        count then project title, so the same input always yields the same ordering.
      </p>
      <p>See <code>JUDGING.md</code> in the repository for the full specification and its limits.</p>

      <h2 id="self-host">Self-hosting</h2>
      <pre>git clone https://github.com/your-org/hackerly
cd hackerly
docker compose up --build</pre>
      <p>
        That brings up a working portal on port 8080 with the schema created, the DOGFOOD fixtures
        loaded and a small demo set seeded. The database is a SQLite file in a volume, so nothing
        depends on an external service and the core product keeps working with the network off.
        Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> to change anything.
      </p>

      <h2 id="architecture">Architecture in one paragraph</h2>
      <p>
        A single Node.js process serves the server-rendered UI and the JSON API. The browser never
        writes to the database; every mutation goes through a service that checks the session, the
        role, the event, the assignment and the deadline. Storage sits behind a driver interface with
        two implementations: SQLite for self-hosted installs, Firestore for a deployment on Google
        Cloud. Firebase Authentication is optional and only used to prove identity — Hackerly
        issues its own server-side session, so authorization never depends on a client-side role.
      </p>

      <h2 id="api">API</h2>
      <p>
        There is a small REST surface under <code>/api/v1</code>: published events, an event&rsquo;s
        tracks and projects, creating an event, creating a submission, reading your own review
        scores, submitting a review, and organizer CSV export. It is documented in
        <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="limits">Honest limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Public voting and comments exist but have no moderation tooling beyond hiding a comment.</li>
        <li>Normalization is optional and off by default. It is documented, not proven.</li>
        <li>There is no email provider by default: judge invitations are written to <code>data/outbox</code> and shown in the organizer&rsquo;s judge list, and the UI says so rather than claiming a message was sent.</li>
        <li>Avatars are generated from a deterministic seed or set to an external HTTPS URL. There is no binary upload pipeline.</li>
        <li>Scale claims are backed only by the load tests in <code>scripts/loadtest.mjs</code>, and their measured numbers are recorded in the README.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

